## Introduction
A **view transform** is a fundamental concept in computer graphics that involves mapping coordinates from one system to another to facilitate the display of graphical content [GFG]. It is essential for presenting elements from a larger virtual "world" onto a smaller physical "screen" or display area [GFG]. This process can involve two-dimensional (2D) transformations, such as mapping a **window** to a **viewport**, or more complex three-dimensional (3D) transformations, known as **3D projections**, which simulate depth and perspective [GFG], [Wiki].

## TL;DR
*   **View transform** maps coordinates from a conceptual "world" to a physical "display" [GFG].
*   In 2D graphics, this is often called **window to viewport transformation**, mapping a rectangular **window** in world coordinates to a **viewport** on the display device [GFG].
*   In 3D graphics, it involves **3D projection** to display three-dimensional objects on a two-dimensional surface, using methods like **perspective projection** or **orthographic projection** [Wiki].
*   It ensures graphics are correctly scaled, positioned, and rendered on the screen [GFG].
*   **QVT (Query/View/Transformations)** is a different concept related to model transformations in software engineering, not graphical view transforms [Wiki].

## Content
### Window to Viewport Transformation

