## Requirements for a Solution
Any effective solution to the Critical-Section Problem must satisfy three fundamental requirements to ensure correctness, efficiency, and fairness [GFG], [Scaler], [TPT]:

### 1. Mutual Exclusion
*   **Definition**: At most, **one process** can be inside its critical section at any given time [GFG], [TP], [Scaler], [TPT].
*   **Purpose**: This prevents conflicts by ensuring that no two processes update the shared resource simultaneously, thereby maintaining data consistency [GFG]. If other processes wish to enter their critical sections, they must wait until the current one is free [TPT].

### 2. Progress
*   **Definition**: If no process is currently in its critical section, and one or more processes want to enter their critical sections, then the choice of which process will enter next should not be postponed indefinitely [GFG], [TP], [Scaler], [TPT].
*   **Purpose**: This ensures that the system continues to make progress rather than getting stuck. Only processes not in their remainder sections can be involved in deciding who enters next [TPT].

### 3. Bounded Waiting
*   **Definition**: There must be a limit on how long a process has to wait before it gets a chance to enter the critical section, once it has requested to do so [GFG], [TP], [Scaler], [TPT].
*   **Purpose**: This requirement prevents **starvation**, a scenario where one process is repeatedly bypassed while others continuously enter and exit the critical section [GFG]. It sets a bound on the number of times other processes can gain access after a process has made a request and before its request is granted [TPT].

## Solutions to the Critical Section Problem
To effectively address the Critical Section Problem, various algorithms and methods have been developed to synchronize different processes [GFG], [TPT]. These solutions generally fall into software-based, hardware-based, or operating system/programming language construct categories.

### 1. Interrupt Disable
This method involves a process disabling all hardware interrupts before entering its critical section and re-enabling them upon exiting [GFG].
*   **Mechanism**: Since no interrupts can occur, the running process cannot be preempted [GFG]. It executes its critical section without interruption, ensuring mutual exclusion on single-processor systems [GFG].
*   **Limitation**: This approach is not suitable for multiprocessor systems and can negatively impact system responsiveness if a process disables interrupts for too long [GFG].

