### 2. Locks
Locks are fundamental synchronization tools that ensure only one process at a time can enter the critical section [GFG].
*   **Mechanism**: A process must "acquire" the lock before entering the critical section and "release" it after exiting [GFG]. If another process tries to enter while the lock is acquired, it must wait [GFG].

#### (a) Software-Based Locks
*   **Mechanism**: These are implemented using algorithms that rely on shared variables (like flags, turn variables, or ticket numbers) to coordinate access to the critical section [GFG].
*   **Examples**: Peterson''s Solution (not detailed in excerpts but an example of a software solution), Bakery Algorithm [GFG].
*   **Limitation**: Often complex to implement correctly and can suffer from **busy waiting** (where a process repeatedly checks if it can enter, wasting CPU cycles) or other issues [GFG].
<p align="center">
  <img src="https://ik.imagekit.io/huwygg3fd/Gemini_Generated_Image_w736d2w736d2w736.png" width="80%">
</p>


#### (b) Hardware-Based Locks
*   **Mechanism**: Modern CPUs provide special **atomic instructions** (indivisible operations that complete entirely without interruption) that make lock implementation easier and faster [GFG]. These instructions typically read a value, modify it, and write it back in a single, uninterruptible CPU cycle.
*   **Examples**:
    *   **Test-and-Set()**: Atomically tests a value and sets it to true.
    *   **Compare-and-Swap()**: Atomically compares a value with an expected value and, if they match, swaps it with a new value.
*   **Advantage**: Much simpler than complex software algorithms and work effectively in multiprocessor systems [GFG]

<p align="center">
  <img src="https://ik.imagekit.io/huwygg3fd/Gemini_Generated_Image_1v9kh21v9kh21v9k.png" width="80%">
</p>


### 3. Mutexes (Mutual Exclusion Locks)
A **mutex** is a higher-level lock abstraction, often built on top of hardware primitives like spinlocks [GFG].
*   **Mechanism**: Similar to a basic lock, a process acquires the mutex before its critical section and releases it afterward [GFG].
*   **Advantage**: Unlike raw locks that might always involve busy waiting, a mutex doesn''t always require busy waiting. If the mutex is unavailable, the process can be blocked (put to sleep) instead of continuously spinning, thus saving CPU cycles [GFG]. When the mutex becomes available, a waiting process is unblocked [GFG].

<p align="center">
  <img src="https://ik.imagekit.io/huwygg3fd/Gemini_Generated_Image_u4uj3qu4uj3qu4uj.png" width="80%">
</p>


### 4. Semaphores
A **semaphore** is an integer variable used for signaling among processes, accessed only through two atomic operations: **wait()** (also known as **P()** or **down()**) and **signal()** (also known as **V()** or **up()**) [GFG].
*   **Mechanism**:
    *   **wait()**: Decrements the semaphore value. If the value becomes negative, the process is blocked (put into a waiting queue) [GFG].
    *   **signal()**: Increments the semaphore value. If the value is less than or equal to zero, it wakes up one waiting process [GFG].
*   **Advantage**: Semaphores remove busy waiting by blocking processes when the critical section is unavailable, improving CPU utilization [GFG].
*   **Types**: Binary semaphores (0 or 1, similar to mutexes) and counting semaphores (for managing multiple instances of a resource).

<p align="center">
  <img src="https://ik.imagekit.io/huwygg3fd/Gemini_Generated_Image_dk5luvdk5luvdk5l.png" width="80%">
</p>


### 5. Monitors
A **monitor** is a higher-level synchronization construct that combines mutual exclusion and condition variables into a single programming language construct [GFG].
*   **Mechanism**:
    *   Only **one process** can execute inside a monitor at a time, automatically ensuring mutual exclusion for procedures defined within it [GFG].
    *   **Condition variables** (`wait()` and `signal()`) are used within monitors to allow processes to wait for certain conditions to be met and to signal other waiting processes when those conditions change [GFG].
*   **Advantage**: Monitors simplify the implementation of complex synchronization problems by encapsulating shared data and operations, making them less error-prone than semaphores [GFG].

<p align="center">
  <img src="https://ik.imagekit.io/huwygg3fd/Gemini_Generated_Image_a5ki3ka5ki3ka5ki.png" width="80%">
</p>

## Examples of Critical Sections in Real-World Applications
The Critical-Section Problem is pervasive in systems where concurrent access to shared data occurs.

*   **Banking System (ATM or Online Banking)** [GFG]:
    *   **Critical Section**: Updating an account balance during a deposit or withdrawal [GFG].
