## Uninformed (Blind) Search Algorithms
Uninformed search algorithms, also known as **blind search**, systematically explore the search space without using any prior knowledge about the problem domain or the location of the goal. They treat all nodes as equally important and select paths based purely on their internal search strategy [GFG], [Scaler]. These strategies typically involve a fixed order of exploring nodes.

### 1. Breadth-First Search (BFS)
**Concept:** BFS is a fundamental search algorithm that explores all possible paths **level by level** [GFG]. It begins from the **root node** (initial state) and explores all its immediate neighboring nodes before moving to the next level of neighbors [GFG], [TP].
**Working Principle:**
*   Starts at the root node.
*   Explores all nodes at the current depth level before moving to nodes at the next depth level [GFG], [TP].
*   Uses a **queue** data structure to store nodes to be visited, ensuring that nodes are processed in FIFO (First-In, First-Out) order [TP].
*   Generates one search tree at a time until the solution is found [TP].
**Properties:**
*   **Completeness:** Yes, if a solution exists, BFS will find it [needs review].
*   **Optimality:** Yes, if all edge costs are uniform (or 1), BFS finds the shortest path [TP]. If costs vary, it''s not guaranteed to be optimal; Uniform Cost Search is needed [needs review].
*   **Time Complexity:** O(b^d), where ''b'' is the branching factor and ''d'' is the depth of the shallowest goal [needs review].
*   **Space Complexity:** O(b^d), can be memory intensive as it stores all nodes at the current level [GFG].

### 2. Depth-First Search (DFS)
**Concept:** DFS explores paths by going as **deep as possible** along one direction (path) before backtracking [GFG].
**Working Principle:**
*   Starts at the root node.
*   Explores a single branch completely until it reaches a leaf node or a dead end.
