## C Structures (structs)

### Need / Why It Is Required
In programming, particularly in languages like C, there''s often a need to represent real-world entities that have multiple, related attributes but these attributes might be of different data types. For example, a student might have a name (string/char array), roll number (integer), and average marks (float). While arrays can group multiple variables of the *same* type, they cannot group different types together efficiently under one logical unit. **C Structures** address this by providing a mechanism to group such disparate but related variables into a single, custom data type [W3Schools, C Structures].

### Definition / Core Concept
A **structure** (often called a **struct**) in C is a user-defined data type that allows you to group several related variables (of potentially different data types) into one logical unit [W3Schools, C Structures]. Each variable within a structure is referred to as a **member** of the structure [W3Schools, C Structures]. Unlike an array, which stores a collection of elements of the same data type, a structure can contain members of various data types [W3Schools, C Structures].

### Working Principle / How It Works
To use a structure, you first need to **define** its blueprint, specifying the name of the structure and the types and names of its members. Then, you can **declare** variables of that structure type and **access** their individual members to assign or retrieve values.

#### Create a Structure
You define a structure using the **`struct`** keyword, followed by the structure''s name and a block of curly braces `{}` containing the declarations of its members [W3Schools, C Structures].

**Syntax:**
```c
struct MyStructure { // Structure declaration
  int myNum;         // Member (int variable)
  char myLetter;     // Member (char variable)
  // Other members can be added here
};
```
[W3Schools, Create a Structure]

This declaration defines a *new data type* named `struct MyStructure`. It does not allocate any memory; it merely tells the compiler what a `MyStructure` variable would look like.

#### Create Structure Variables
Once the structure is defined, you can declare variables of that structure type. Each variable will have its own set of members.

**Example:**
```c
// Create a structure variable of MyStructure called s1
struct MyStructure s1;
```
[W3Schools, Example]

Here, `s1` is a variable of type `struct MyStructure`.

#### Access Structure Members
To access individual members of a structure variable, you use the **dot syntax (`.`)** [W3Schools, Access Structure Members].

**Example:**
```c
// Create a structure called myStructure
struct myStructure {
  int myNum;
  char myLetter;
};

int main() {
  // Create a structure variable of myStructure called s1
  struct myStructure s1;

  // Assign values to members of s1
  s1.myNum = 13;
  s1.myLetter = ''B'';

  // Print the values
  printf("My number: %d\n", s1.myNum);
  printf("My letter: %c\n", s1.myLetter);

  return 0;
}
```
**Output:**
```
My number: 13
My letter: B
```
[W3Schools, Example]

