## TL;DR / Quick Summary

*   **Definition**: Sequential search, also known as **linear search**, checks each item in a list one by one until the target item is found or the end of the list is reached [TP].
*   **Simplicity**: It is one of the simplest search algorithms and is easy to understand and implement [TP].
*   **Data Requirement**: Can be used on both **sorted** and **unsorted** arrays/lists and with any data type [GFG].
*   **Time Complexity**:
    *   **Best Case**: O(1) (element found at the first position) [GFG], [TP], [TPT].
    *   **Worst Case**: O(N) (element at the last position or not found) [GFG], [TP], [TPT].
*   **Space Complexity**: O(1) (requires no additional memory) [GFG].
*   **Use Cases**: Best suited for **small datasets** or **unsorted lists** [GFG], [TP].
*   **Variants**: Includes **Sentinel Linear Search** (optimizes loop condition) and **Indexed Sequential Search** (uses a sorted index for faster block-wise search on larger, sorted data) [GFG].

## Introduction

Sequential search, commonly referred to as **linear search**, is a fundamental and intuitive algorithm used to find a specific item within a collection of data. At its core, it involves examining each element in the list sequentially until the desired item is located or the entire list has been traversed [TP]. Its simplicity makes it a foundational concept in computer science and a practical choice for specific scenarios, despite its performance limitations with large datasets [TP]. This method is robust, capable of operating on various data types and without requiring the data to be sorted [GFG]. This document will explore its definition, working mechanism, performance characteristics, practical applications, and common variants like Sentinel Linear Search and Indexed Sequential Search.

## Definition / Core Concept

**Sequential Search** is a method of finding an item by checking each entry in a list one by one [TP]. It systematically traverses every element within an input array, comparing each element to a target value, referred to as the **search key** or **key element** [TPT]. If the item exists in the list, the search stops, and its location (index) is returned. Otherwise, if the item is not found after checking all entries, the search concludes, indicating the item''s absence [TP]. It is also widely known as **linear search** [TPT].

## Types of Sequential Search

While the term "sequential search" often refers to basic linear search, there are important variations designed for specific optimizations or data structures.

### Linear Search

This is the most straightforward implementation of sequential search. It involves iterating through the list from beginning to end, comparing each element with the **key element** [TPT].
*   **Characteristics**:
    *   Can be used irrespective of whether the array is sorted or not [GFG].
    *   Can be used on arrays of any data type [GFG].
    *   Does not require any additional memory [GFG].

<p align="center">
  <img src="https://ik.imagekit.io/qmokucfbj/85.png" width="80%">
</p>

### Sentinel Linear Search

The basic idea of Sentinel Linear Search is to eliminate the conditional check for the array''s boundary within the loop, which can offer minor performance improvements by reducing comparisons per iteration [GFG].
*   **Mechanism**: An extra element, called a **sentinel value**, matching the **search key** is temporarily added to the end of the array [GFG].
*   **Benefit**: This modification means the loop condition only needs to check if the current element matches the key, as the loop is guaranteed to terminate when the sentinel (which matches the key) is eventually reached if the key wasn''t found earlier [GFG].

<p align="center">
  <img src="https://ik.imagekit.io/qmokucfbj/86.png" width="80%">
</p>

### Indexed Sequential Search

This variant is specifically designed for scenarios where the data is **sorted** and potentially large, aiming to improve performance over a pure linear scan [GFG].
*   **Characteristics**:
    *   A **sorted index** is maintained in addition to the main array [GFG].
    *   Each element in this index points to a **block of elements** in the main array or another expanded index [GFG].
    *   The search first involves searching the (much smaller) index to identify the relevant block. Once the block is found, a linear search is performed within that specific block [GFG].
    *   This approach combines characteristics of indexing and sequential searching.

<p align="center">
  <img src="https://ik.imagekit.io/qmokucfbj/87.png" width="80%">
</p>

## How Sequential Search Works (Algorithm Steps & Pseudocode)

The fundamental process of sequential search is simple and involves a direct comparison of elements.

### Basic Linear Search Algorithm

1.  **Start**: Begin the search from the **0th index** (the very first element) of the input array [TPT].
2.  **Compare**: Compare the **key element** (the value to be searched) with the element at the current index [TPT].
3.  **Match Found**: If a match is found (current element == key element), the search stops, and the **index (location)** of the element is returned [TPT].
4.  **No Match**: If no match is found, move to the next element in the array by incrementing the index [TPT].
5.  **End of List**: Repeat steps 2-4 until either the key element is found or the end of the array is reached [TPT].
6.  **Element Not Present**: If the end of the array is reached and the key element has not been found, it indicates that the element is not present in the array [TPT].

<p align="center">
  <img src="https://ik.imagekit.io/qmokucfbj/88.png" width="80%">
</p>

### Pseudocode for Linear Search

```
procedure linear_search (list, value)
    for each item in the list
        if match item == value
            return the item''s location
        end if
    end for
    return "Not Found" // Or a specific indicator like -1
end procedure
```
[TPT]

### Flowchart for Linear Search

```mermaid
graph TD
    A[Start] --> B{Initialize index i = 0};
    B --> C{Is i < length of list?};
    C -- No --> D[Return "Not Found"];
    C -- Yes --> E{Is list[i] == target value?};
    E -- Yes --> F[Return i (Index Found)];
    E -- No --> G[Increment i];
    G --> C;
```

### Sentinel Linear Search Steps

1.  **Initialize**: Initialize the search index variable `i` to 0 [GFG].
2.  **Set Sentinel**: Set the **last element** of the array to the **search key** [GFG]. This is the sentinel.
3.  **Loop and Compare**: While the current element `list[i]` is **not equal to** the search key, increment `i` [GFG].
4.  **Check for Original Key**: After the loop terminates, check if `i` is less than the original last index of the array (before the sentinel was placed).
    *   If `i` is less than the original last index, it means the key was found within the original array elements. Return `i`.
    *   If `i` is equal to the original last index, it means the loop terminated because it hit the sentinel (which was the key), implying the key was not found in the original array. Return "Not Found" or equivalent.

<p align="center">
  <img src="https://ik.imagekit.io/qmokucfbj/89.png" width="80%">
</p>

## Analysis and Performance

The performance of sequential search is primarily evaluated based on the number of comparisons it makes.

### Time Complexity

*   **Best Case**: O(1)
    *   Occurs when the **key element** is found at the **first index** of the list [GFG], [TP], [TPT]. Only one comparison is needed.
*   **Worst Case**: O(N)
    *   Occurs when the **key element** is present at the **last index** of the list, or when the element is **not present** in the list at all [GFG], [TP], [TPT]. In this scenario, the algorithm must traverse through all **N** elements.
*   **Average Case**: O(N)
    *   On average, the algorithm is expected to examine approximately half of the elements (N/2) to find the key. Since constant factors are ignored in Big O notation, the average case complexity is also O(N). The time taken to search grows linearly with the number of entries [TP].

### Space Complexity

*   **O(1)**
    *   Sequential search does **not require any additional memory** beyond the storage of the input array itself [GFG]. It performs in-place comparisons.

### Limitations / Disadvantages

*   **Slow for Large Datasets**: Sequential search has a time complexity of O(N), which makes it slow for **large datasets** [GFG], [TP]. The time it takes to search grows linearly with the number of entries [TP].
*   **Not Suitable for Large Arrays**: It is generally **not suitable for large arrays** where performance is critical [GFG].

## Advantages

Sequential search offers several benefits, particularly in specific contexts:

*   **Flexibility**: It can be used **irrespective of whether the array is sorted or not** [GFG]. This removes the preprocessing step of sorting, which can be expensive for dynamic data.
*   **Versatility**: It can be used on arrays of **any data type** (e.g., integers, strings, objects, as long as a comparison operator is defined) [GFG].
*   **Memory Efficiency**: It does **not require any additional memory** (O(1) space complexity), making it highly memory-efficient [GFG].
*   **Simplicity**: The algorithm is **straightforward and easy to understand**, implement, and debug [TP].
*   **No Special Setup**: It **does not require any advanced data structures** or complex setup procedures, making it easy to integrate into existing systems [TP].
*   **Well-suited for Small Datasets**: It is a well-suited algorithm for **small datasets** where the overhead of more complex algorithms might outweigh their performance benefits [GFG].

## Applications and When to Use

Sequential search, despite its limitations for large datasets, has practical applications in specific scenarios:

*   **Unsorted Lists**: It is most commonly used to find an element in an **unsorted array or list**, as no other faster method (like binary search) can be applied without sorting first [GFG].
