## TL;DR

*   A **Parse Tree**, also known as a **Syntax Tree** or **Derivation Tree**, is a hierarchical, tree-like representation of how a string is derived according to a **formal grammar** (specifically, a **Context-Free Grammar** or **CFG**) [GFG], [Wiki], [TP].
*   It visually displays the syntactic structure of an input string [GFG], [TP].
*   **Rules for construction**: **Leaf nodes** must be **terminals**, **interior nodes** must be **non-terminals**, and an **in-order traversal** of the tree yields the original input string [GFG]. The root is always the **start symbol** [GFG_PT_ST].
*   Used extensively in **compiler design** for **syntax analysis**, helping to ensure source code conforms to language rules [GFG], [TP].
*   Parsers are broadly categorized as **Top-down** (root to leaves) or **Bottom-up** (leaves to root) based on how they construct the parse tree [TP].
*   A **grammar is ambiguous** if a single string can have multiple distinct parse trees, posing challenges in parsing [TP].
*   Differs from a **Syntax Tree** (or **Abstract Syntax Tree - AST**) which is a condensed, simplified representation, ignoring non-essential syntactic details for easier semantic analysis and code generation [GFG_PT_ST], [GFG_AST].

## Introduction

Parsing is a fundamental process in the fields of **automata theory** and **compiler design**, concerned with analyzing a string of symbols to determine if it conforms to the grammatical rules of a formal language [TP]. The **Parse Tree** is a critical tool in this process, providing a structured, graphical representation of how an input string is derived from the **start symbol** of a **Context-Free Grammar (CFG)** [TP], [GFG_PT_ST].

These trees are essential for visualizing and analyzing the syntactic structure of source code, helping compilers understand the structure of a program and ensuring it adheres to the language''s grammar [GFG].

<p align="center">
  <img src="https://ik.imagekit.io/0bl3glpdy/Gemini_Generated_Image_xh34ikxh34ikxh34.png" width="60%">
</p>

## Definition / Core Concept

A **Parse Tree**, also referred to as a **syntax tree** or **derivation tree**, is a hierarchical, tree-like structure that represents the derivation of a string based on the production rules of a **formal grammar** [GFG], [Wiki], [TP]. Specifically, it illustrates how a **Context-Free Grammar (CFG)** is used to generate a given input string [GFG], [TP]. The **start symbol** of the grammar always serves as the **root** of the parse tree [GFG_PT_ST].

## Need / Why It Is Required

In compiler design, the syntax analysis phase (parsing) is responsible for checking if the program written by the user follows the syntax rules of the language. The parse tree provides a concrete, visual, and structured representation of this syntactic analysis [GFG]. Without such a representation, it would be challenging for the compiler to:
*   Understand the hierarchical relationships between different parts of the code.
*   Identify syntax errors effectively.
*   Proceed to subsequent compilation phases like semantic analysis and code generation, which often rely on this structural understanding [GFG_PT_ST].

Parse trees serve as an **in-memory representation** of the input, structured according to the grammar, making it an indispensable tool for confirming the correct syntactic structure of programs [GFG].

## Basics of Context-Free Grammar (CFG)

Before delving into parse trees, it is crucial to understand **Context-Free Grammar (CFG)**. A CFG is a formal grammar in which every production rule is of the form **A → β**, where **A** is a single **non-terminal symbol**, and **β** is a string of **terminals** and/or **non-terminals** [TP]. CFGs are used to describe all possible strings in a given formal language and are fundamental for defining the syntax of programming languages [TP]. Parse trees are specifically used to parse **context-free languages** [TP].

## Rules to Draw a Parse Tree

To construct a valid parse tree for a given string and grammar, the following rules must be adhered to:
*   All **leaf nodes** in the parse tree must be **terminal symbols** from the grammar [GFG].
*   All **interior nodes** (non-leaf nodes) in the parse tree must be **non-terminal symbols** from the grammar [GFG].
*   The **root node** of the parse tree is always the **start symbol** of the grammar [GFG_PT_ST].
*   The children of any interior node represent the symbols on the right-hand side of a production rule used to expand that non-terminal [GFG].
*   An **in-order traversal** of the leaf nodes of the parse tree will yield the original input string that the tree represents [GFG].

## Role in Parsing

Parse trees play a central role in parsing by offering a graphical representation of the **derivation** of a string [TP]. They visually show how the **start symbol** of a CFG is successively transformed into the final input string through the application of a sequence of **production rules** [TP]. This visual derivation allows for clear understanding of the syntactic structure and verification against the grammar.

## Types of Parsers

Parsers, the components that construct parse trees, can be broadly classified into two main types based on their approach to building the tree:

1.  **Top-down Parsers**:
    *   These parsers begin building the parse tree from the **root node** (the start symbol) and proceed downwards towards the **leaf nodes** (the terminal symbols of the input string) [TP].
    *   They attempt to find the leftmost derivation of the input string.
    *   Examples include Recursive Descent Parsers and LL Parsers.

2.  **Bottom-up Parsers**:
    *   These parsers start with the **leaf nodes** (the input string''s terminal symbols) and work their way upwards towards the **root node** (the start symbol) [TP].
    *   They try to reduce the input string to the start symbol by applying production rules in reverse.
    *   Examples include Shift-Reduce Parsers and LR Parsers.

