  <img src="https://ik.imagekit.io/fm4gom0qc/Gemini_Generated_Image_ul53twul53twul53.png" width="80%">
</p>


## Bandwidth (BW)

The **Bandwidth (BW)** of a resonant circuit is defined as the range of frequencies over which the circuit''s response (current or output voltage) is at least 70.7% (or $1/\sqrt{2}$) of its maximum value at the resonant frequency [Testbook]. These frequencies, where the power delivered to the resistor is half the maximum power, are known as **half-power frequencies** or **cutoff frequencies**.

### Half-Power Frequencies
*   **Lower Cutoff Frequency ($f_1$)**: The frequency below $f_0$ where the circuit''s response falls to $1/\sqrt{2}$ (or 70.7%) of its maximum value. Power is half the maximum.
*   **Upper Cutoff Frequency ($f_2$)**: The frequency above $f_0$ where the circuit''s response falls to $1/\sqrt{2}$ (or 70.7%) of its maximum value. Power is half the maximum.

