## PEAS Representation of AI Agent

**PEAS** stands for **Performance measure**, **Environment**, **Actuators**, and **Sensors** [GFG]. It is a structured framework used to describe and understand an AI agent, providing a comprehensive approach to its design [GFG].

*   **P - Performance Measure**: This defines the criteria for evaluating the success of the agent''s behavior. It quantifies how "good" an agent''s actions are [GFG]. For example, in a self-driving car, performance measures could include safety (no accidents), efficiency (time to destination), comfort (smooth ride), and legality (obeying traffic laws) [needs review - example context for performance measure not explicitly in source].
*   **E - Environment**: This describes the specific context in which the agent operates. It defines the boundaries and characteristics of the world the agent perceives and acts upon [GFG]. For example, for a self-driving car, the environment includes roads, other traffic, pedestrians, weather conditions, and traffic signs [needs review - example context for environment not explicitly in source].
*   **A - Actuators**: These are the means by which the agent performs actions in the environment [GFG]. For a self-driving car, actuators include the steering wheel, accelerator, brakes, and turn signals [needs review - example context for actuators not explicitly in source].
*   **S - Sensors**: These are the inputs the agent uses to perceive the environment [GFG]. For a self-driving car, sensors include cameras, radar, lidar, GPS, and odometers [needs review - example context for sensors not explicitly in source].

## Rational Agents and Rationality in Decision-Making

Intelligent agents are characterized by their **rationality in decision-making** [GFG]. A **rational agent** aims to attain optimal outcomes or, in uncertain scenarios, the best-expected outcome [GFG]. This means the agent chooses actions that are expected to maximize its performance measure given its percept sequence and its built-in knowledge [GFG]. Rationality doesn''t necessarily mean perfection, but rather acting appropriately to achieve the best possible result under given circumstances and available information [GFG].

## Types / Classification of Intelligent Agents

Intelligent agents can be classified based on their level of complexity, internal decision-making mechanisms, and ability to learn or adapt [TP], [GFG].

The classification forms a hierarchy, with more complex agents building upon the capabilities of simpler ones.

