## TL;DR / Quick Summary

*   **National Income** measures the total value of final goods and services produced by a country''s residents within a specific period, usually a year [Wiki].
*   It''s a key indicator of a nation''s economic performance and living standards.
*   The **Circular Flow of Income** illustrates the continuous movement of money and goods/services in an economy [GFG].
*   **Aggregates** like **GDP** (Gross Domestic Product) and **GNP** (Gross National Product) are used to measure national income.
    *   **Gross vs. Net**: Difference is **Depreciation** (consumption of fixed capital).
    *   **Domestic vs. National**: Difference is **Net Factor Income from Abroad (NFIA)**.
    *   **Market Price vs. Factor Cost**: Difference is **Net Indirect Taxes (NIT)**.
*   **National Income** is most commonly referred to as **Net National Product at Factor Cost (NNP FC)** [GFG, by implication].
*   Three main **methods of calculation**:
    1.  **Value Added Method**: Sum of value added at each production stage [GFG].
    2.  **Income Method**: Sum of all factor payments (wages, rent, interest, profit) [GFG].
    3.  **Expenditure Method**: Sum of all final expenditures (consumption, investment, government spending, net exports) [GFG].

## Introduction

National Income is a fundamental concept in macroeconomics, representing the total monetary value of all final goods and services produced by a country within a specific period, typically a financial year [Wiki]. It provides a comprehensive measure of economic activity, offering insights into the economic health, growth, and living standards of a nation [Wiki]. Understanding national income helps policymakers formulate economic strategies, assess economic performance, and compare development across different countries [Wiki].

## Definition / Core Concept

**National Income** refers to the total value of all final goods and services produced by the normal residents of a country during an accounting year, valued at factor cost. It essentially represents the sum of factor incomes (wages, rent, interest, profit) earned by the residents for their contribution to production [GFG].
In standard national income accounting, **National Income** is synonymously used with **Net National Product at Factor Cost (NNP FC)** [GFG, by implication when defining Domestic Income (NDP FC)].

## Circular Flow of Income

The **circular flow of income** illustrates the continuous movement of money and goods/services between different sectors of an economy, such as households, firms, government, and the external sector [GFG]. It shows how income is generated, distributed, and spent, highlighting the interdependencies within an economy [GFG].

<p align="center">
  <img src="https://ik.imagekit.io/6vkdqnalh/unnamedghghjg.jpg" width="80%">
</p>

```mermaid
graph LR
    A[Households] -- Factor Services (Land, Labor, Capital, Entrepreneurship) --> B(Firms)
    B -- Goods & Services --> A
    B -- Factor Payments (Wages, Rent, Interest, Profit) --> A
    A -- Consumption Expenditure --> B

    A -- Taxes --> C(Government)
    B -- Taxes --> C
    C -- Government Expenditure & Transfers --> A
    C -- Government Purchases & Subsidies --> B

    A -- Imports --> D(Rest of the World)
    B -- Imports --> D
    D -- Exports --> A
    D -- Exports --> B
    D -- Net Factor Income from Abroad --> A
```
*   **Households** provide factor services to firms and receive factor payments, which they use for consumption, saving, and paying taxes [GFG].
*   **Firms** produce goods and services using factor services from households, pay factor payments, and sell their output to households, government, and the external sector [GFG].
*   **Government** collects taxes from households and firms and incurs expenditure on public goods and services, as well as providing transfers and subsidies [GFG].
*   **Rest of the World** represents the external sector, involving exports, imports, and net factor income from abroad [GFG].

## Basic Aggregates of National Income

A wide variety of goods and services are produced in an economy over a year by various production units [GFG]. Since these goods and services cannot be added in terms of their physical quantities (e.g., adding cars and apples), their **monetary value** is used for aggregation [GFG]. To avoid **double counting** (counting the value of intermediate goods multiple times), only the value of **final goods and services** is considered when calculating national income aggregates [GFG].

## Measures of National Income and Output

National income is measured using various aggregates, each providing a different perspective on economic activity. These aggregates are interrelated through three main adjustments:

1.  **Gross vs. Net**: The difference between gross and net measures is **Depreciation** (also known as Consumption of Fixed Capital).
    *   **Gross** measures include depreciation.
    *   **Net** measures exclude depreciation.
    *   **Net = Gross - Depreciation**
    *   **Gross = Net + Depreciation**
2.  **Domestic vs. National**: The difference between domestic and national measures is **Net Factor Income from Abroad (NFIA)**.
    *   **Domestic** measures production within the domestic territory, irrespective of who produces it.
    *   **National** measures production by normal residents, irrespective of where it is produced.
    *   **National = Domestic + NFIA**
    *   **Domestic = National - NFIA**
    *   **NFIA = Factor Income from Abroad - Factor Income to Abroad**
3.  **Market Price vs. Factor Cost**: The difference between market price and factor cost measures is **Net Indirect Taxes (NIT)**.
    *   **Market Price** includes indirect taxes and excludes subsidies.
    *   **Factor Cost** reflects the cost of factors of production.
    *   **Factor Cost = Market Price - NIT**
    *   **Market Price = Factor Cost + NIT**
    *   **NIT = Indirect Taxes - Subsidies**

### Gross Domestic Product (GDP)

**Gross Domestic Product (GDP)** measures the total value of all final goods and services produced within the **domestic territory** of a country during an accounting year [GFG].

#### Gross Domestic Product at Market Price (GDP MP )
**GDP MP** refers to the gross market value of all the final goods and services produced during a year within the domestic territory of a country [GFG]. It represents the total expenditure incurred on final goods and services produced within the economy [GFG].

#### Gross Domestic Product at Factor Cost (GDP FC )
**GDP FC** refers to the gross money value of all the final goods and services produced during a year within the domestic territory of a country [GFG].
**GDP FC = GDP MP - Net Indirect Taxes (NIT)** [GFG]

### Net Domestic Product (NDP)

**Net Domestic Product (NDP)** adjusts GDP for depreciation. It measures the net value of production within the domestic territory.

#### Net Domestic Product at Market Price (NDP MP )
**NDP MP** refers to the net market value of all the final goods and services produced during a year within the domestic territory of a country [GFG].
**NDP MP = GDP MP - Depreciation** [GFG]

#### Net Domestic Product at Factor Cost (NDP FC )
**NDP FC** refers to the net money value of all the final goods and services produced during a year within the domestic territory of a country [GFG].
**NDP FC** is also known as **Domestic Factor Income** or **Domestic Income** [GFG].
**NDP FC = NDP MP - Net Indirect Taxes (NIT)** [GFG]
**NDP FC = GDP FC - Depreciation** [GFG]

### Gross National Product (GNP)

**Gross National Product (GNP)** measures the total value of all final goods and services produced by the **normal residents** of a country, regardless of where they are located [GFG].

#### Gross National Product at Market Price (GNP MP )
**GNP MP** refers to the gross market value of all the final goods and services produced during a year by the normal residents of a country [GFG].
**GNP MP = GDP MP + Net Factor Income from Abroad (NFIA)** [GFG]
A country''s GNP MP can be less than its GDP MP if NFIA is negative (factor income paid abroad is greater than factor income received from abroad) [GFG].

#### Gross National Product at Factor Cost (GNP FC )
**GNP FC** refers to the gross money value of all the final goods and services produced during a year by the normal residents of a country [GFG].
**GNP FC = GNP MP - Net Indirect Taxes (NIT)** [GFG]
**GNP FC = GDP FC + Net Factor Income from Abroad (NFIA)** [GFG]

### Net National Product (NNP)

**Net National Product (NNP)** adjusts GNP for depreciation.

#### Net National Product at Market Price (NNP MP)
**NNP MP = GNP MP - Depreciation** [Derived from definitions]
This represents the net market value of all final goods and services produced by normal residents.

#### Net National Product at Factor Cost (NNP FC)
**NNP FC = GNP FC - Depreciation** [Derived from definitions]
**NNP FC = NDP FC + Net Factor Income from Abroad (NFIA)** [Derived from definitions]
This aggregate, **NNP FC**, is universally regarded as the **National Income** of a country [GFG, by implication from NDP FC definition and the general understanding of National Income].

## Formula for National Income (Byjus Method)

A common representation of national income, particularly from an expenditure perspective with adjustments, is given as:
**National income = C + G + I + X + F – D** [Byjus]

Where:
*   **C** denotes **consumption** [Byjus]
*   **G** denotes **government expenditure** [Byjus]
*   **I** denotes **investments** [Byjus]
*   **X** denotes **net exports** (Exports subtracted by imports) [Byjus]
*   **F** denotes **Net Factor Income from Abroad (NFIA)** [needs review - interpreted from context as converting domestic to national]
*   **D** denotes **Depreciation** [needs review - interpreted from context as converting gross to net]

**Note**: In standard national income accounting, `C + G + I + X` typically gives **GDP MP**. Adding `F` (NFIA) would yield **GNP MP**. Subtracting `D` (Depreciation) would then result in **NNP MP**. To arrive at **National Income (NNP FC)**, one would additionally need to subtract **Net Indirect Taxes (NIT)** from NNP MP. The formula provided by Byjus sp...', '2025-12-27 08:33:11.452346+00', '2026-01-19 09:58:22.742601+00', ARRAY["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/World_Bank_income_groups%2C_2023.png/500px-World_Bank_income_groups%2C_2023.png","https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/HD%40DH.nrw_Karton_offen.svg/120px-HD%40DH.nrw_Karton_offen.svg.png","https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5d/Flag_of_the_United_States_%2823px%29.png","https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Flag_of_the_People%27s_Republic_of_China.svg/40px-Flag_of_the_People%27s_Republic_of_China.svg.png","https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/9e/Flag_of_Japan.svg/40px-Flag_of_Japan.svg.png","https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/ba/Flag_of_Germany.svg/40px-Flag_of_Germany.svg.png","https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/96/Symbol_category_class.svg/20px-Symbol_category_class.svg.png","https://cdn1.byjus.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/Vector-2219-2.png","https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20220804124322/22-660x436.jpg","https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20251111155821569558/aggregates_of_national_income.webp","https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/cdn-uploads/20220829145701/Basic-Aggregates-of-National-Income-1-2.jpg","https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/cdn-uploads/20220829150124/Basic-Aggregates-of-National-Income-2-11.jpg","https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20220822113931/BasicAggregatesofNationalIncome33.jpg","https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20230613162153/Value-Added-1.png","https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-