## Introduction

The **Entity-Relationship (ER) model** is a high-level conceptual data model that helps in designing a database system [GFG]. It represents the logical view of a system from a data perspective [GFG]. During the database design process, requirements are gathered, and then a logical or conceptual model, often an ER diagram, is created [GFG]. ER diagrams effectively represent real-world objects and their relationships, which can then be easily converted into relational tables [GFG].

**Constraints** play a crucial role in the ER model by defining rules and limitations on the relationships between entities [TP]. These limitations are essential for ensuring the integrity and consistency of the data stored in the database [GFG]. Understanding constraints is fundamental for designing robust and accurate database schemas.

## TL;DR

*   **ER Model** represents real-world entities and their relationships.
*   **Constraints** are rules or limitations on these relationships, crucial for data integrity.
*   **Structural Constraints** combine Cardinality Ratios and Participation Constraints [GFG].
*   **Mapping Cardinality** (Cardinality Ratios) defines the number of entities an entity can be associated with in a relationship [TP]. Types: **One-to-One (1:1)**, **One-to-Many (1:N)**, **Many-to-One (N:1)**, **Many-to-Many (M:N)** [GFG], [TP].
*   **Participation Constraints** specify whether an entity''s participation in a relationship is mandatory or optional [GFG]. Types: **Total Participation** (every entity participates) and **Partial Participation** (some entities may not participate) [GFG], [TP].
*   Other important database constraints include **Domain**, **Key**, **Entity Integrity**, and **Referential Integrity** constraints [GFG].

## ER Model Basics

The ER model uses several fundamental concepts to represent real-world data:

*   **Entity**: An entity represents a real-world object, concept, or thing about which data is stored in a database [GFG]. In a relational database, tables typically represent these entities [GFG].
*   **Entity Set**: An entity set is a collection of all entities of a particular type [GFG]. For example, a "Student" entity set would contain all individual student entities (e.g., Student E1, Student E2, etc.) [GFG].
*   **Strong Entity**: A strong entity is an entity type that possesses a **key attribute** capable of uniquely identifying each instance of the entity [GFG]. It does not rely on any other entity in the schema for its unique identification [GFG].
*   **Weak Entity**: A weak entity cannot be uniquely identified by its own attributes alone [GFG]. It depends on a **strong entity** (called an identifying entity) to establish its identity [GFG].

## Constraints in ER Model

Constraints are fundamental rules or restrictions placed on the database contents to maintain data quality and consistency [GFG]. They model limitations on the relationships between entities [TP].

### Definition
Constraints in the ER model are rules that limit the number of entities that can be associated with other entities via a relationship set, and whether an entity *must* participate in a relationship [TP], [GFG].

### Types of Constraints
The two primary types of constraints directly applied to relationships in the ER model are:
1.  **Mapping Cardinality (Cardinality Ratios)** [TP], [GFG]
2.  **Participation Constraints** [TP], [GFG]

Together, these are known as **Structural Constraints** [GFG].

## Structural Constraints

**Structural Constraints** are also referred to as structural properties within a database management system (DBMS) [GFG]. They encompass both **Cardinality Ratios** and **Participation Constraints**, defining the allowed structure of relationships between entity sets [GFG].

```mermaid
graph TD
    A[Structural Constraints] --> B{Cardinality Ratios}
    A --> C{Participation Constraints}
    B --> B1[One-to-One (1:1)]
    B --> B2[One-to-Many (1:N)]
    B --> B3[Many-to-One (N:1)]
    B --> B4[Many-to-Many (M:N)]
    C --> C1[Total Participation]
    C --> C2[Partial Participation]
```

### Mapping Cardinality (Cardinality Ratios)

**Mapping Cardinality**, also known as Cardinality Ratios, expresses the number of entities to which another entity can be associated through a relationship set [TP]. For a binary relationship set involving entity sets A and B, the mapping cardinality can be of four types [TP].

The four types of cardinality are:

*   **One-to-One (1:1)**:
    *   In a 1:1 relationship, one entity from entity set A is associated with at most one entity from entity set B, and one entity from entity set B is associated with at most one entity from entity set A [TP].
    *   This means each entity in each entity set participates at most once in the relationship [GFG].
    *   *Example*: A "Person" can have at most one "Passport", and a "Passport" belongs to at most one "Person".

*   **One-to-Many (1:N)**:
    *   In a 1:N relationship, an entity from entity set A can be associated with any number of entities (including zero) from entity set B [TP].
    *   However, an entity from entity set B is associated with at most one entity from entity set A [TP].
    *   *Example*: A "Department" can have many "Employees", but an "Employee" belongs to at most one "Department".

*   **Many-to-One (N:1)**:
    *   In an N:1 relationship, an entity from entity set A can be associated with at most one entity from entity set B [TP].
    *   Conversely, an entity from entity set B can be associated with any number of entities (including zero) from entity set A [TP].
    *   This is the reverse of a 1:N relationship.
    *   *Example*: Many "Students" can enroll in at most one "Course", but a "Course" can have many "Students".

*   **Many-to-Many (M:N)**:
    *   In an M:N relationship, an entity from entity set A can be associated with any number of entities (including zero) from entity set B [TP].
    *   Similarly, an entity from entity set B can be associated with any number of entities (including zero) from entity set A [TP].
    *   *Example*: Many "Students" can enroll in many "Courses", and many "Courses" can have many "Students" enrolled.

```mermaid
graph TD
    subgraph One-to-One (1:1)
        A1(Entity A) -- "associates with at most 1" --> B1(Entity B)
        B1 -- "associates with at most 1" --> A1
    end

    subgraph One-to-Many (1:N)
        A2(Entity A) -- "associates with many (0..N)" --> B2(Entity B)
        B2 -- "associates with at most 1" --> A2
    end

    subgraph Many-to-One (N:1)
        A3(Entity A) -- "associates with at most 1" --> B3(Entity B)
        B3 -- "associates with many (0..N)" --> A3
    end

    subgraph Many-to-Many (M:N)
        A4(Entity A) -- "associates with many (0..N)" --> B4(Entity B)
        B4 -- "associates with many (0..N)" --> A4
    end
```

### Participation Constraints

**Participation Constraints** dictate whether the participation of entities in a relationship is mandatory or optional [GFG]. These constraints are primarily of two types: Total Participation and Partial Participation [GFG], [TP].

*   **Total Participation**:
    *   When **each entity** in an entity set is required to participate in at least one relationship instance within a given relationship set, it is called **Total Participation** [GFG], [TP].
    *   In ER modeling, this implies that instances of the entity must necessarily participate [GFG].
    *   *Example*: Every "Loan" must be associated with a "Customer" [TP]. Every "Employee" *must* work for a "Department".

*   **Partial Participation**:
    *   When **some entities** in a given entity set do not participate in a particular relationship, it is called **Partial Participation** [GFG].
    *   Contrary to total participation, partial participation allows some components of the entity set to not be involved in the relationship [GFG].
    *   *Example*: A "Customer" might have a "Loan" or might not [TP]. Not every "Employee" is a "Manager" (i.e., participates in a "Manages" relationship).

```mermaid
graph LR
    subgraph Total Participation
        E1_total[Entity Set E]
        E1_total -- "must participate" --> R1[Relationship R]
    end

    subgraph Partial Participation
        E2_partial[Entity Set E]
        E2_partial -- "may or may not participate" --> R2[Relationship R]
    end
```

## Other Related Constraints (Relational Database Context)

While the above are specific to the ER model''s conceptual design, database constraints in general are crucial for maintaining data integrity in the underlying relational database model [GFG]. These are restrictions or rules imposed on the database contents to validate its quality and operations like insertion and update [GFG].

*   **Domain Constraints**:
    *   Every domain (column) must contain atomic (smallest indivisible) values [GFG].
    *   Composite and multi-valued attributes are generally not allowed at the relational schema level [GFG].
    *   This constraint ensures a datatype check, confirming that assigned data values conform to their defined data types [GFG].

*   **Key Constraints (Uniqueness Constraints)**:
    *   These constraints ensure that every tuple (row) in a relation (table) is unique [GFG].
    *   A relation can have multiple keys, known as **candidate keys** (minimal superkeys) [GFG].
    *   From these candidate keys, one is chosen as the **primary key** to uniquely identify each tuple [GFG].

*   **Entity Integrity Constraints**:
    *   This constraint states that no **primary key** can take a **NULL** value [GFG].
    *   The primary key''s purpose is to uniquely identify each tuple, and a NULL value would violate this unique identification [GFG].
    *   *Example*: In an Employee table, the EmployeeID (primary key) cannot be NULL [GFG].

*   **Referential Integrity Constraints**:
    *   This constraint is specified between two relations (tables) and is used to maintain consistency among tuples in those relations [GFG].
    *   It is enforced through a **foreign key**, which is an attribute or set of attributes in one relation that refers to the primary key of another relation [GFG].
